'''''Vila de Llevant, Tramuntana''''' ({{lang|ca|Vila de Llevant}}) is a village in the [[La Serra de Llevant]] comarca of [[Tramuntana Province]], [[Balboa]]. With a population of 8,532 (2026 estimate), it is the comarca capital and is situated on the eastern shore of [[Lake Iwarakí]]. The village serves as the administrative centre for the comarca, which encompasses the far eastern highlands of Tramuntana Province along the border with [[Nurra Province|Nurra]].

== Geography ==

Vila de Llevant is situated on the eastern shore of [[Lake Iwarakí]], a reservoir lake formed by the [[Gran Presa del Nord]] dam on the lake's southern outflow. The village occupies a narrow strip of level ground between the lake shore and the steep slopes of the [[Yalabirga Mountain Range|Yalabirga]] range. The surrounding terrain is rugged montane highland, with forested ridges rising above the lake.

The climate is cooler than the coastal lowlands, with a pronounced wet season from May to November. The lake moderates local temperatures, and the surrounding highlands receive substantial rainfall, supporting montane forest vegetation.

== Economy ==

The economy of Vila de Llevant is based primarily on subsistence agriculture, with most residents engaged in small-scale farming on the limited arable land along the lake shore and surrounding highland valleys. The cultivation of maize, beans, root vegetables, and coffee provides the primary means of subsistence for the majority of the population.

The village has a minor tourism sector centred on Lake Iwarakí, attracting visitors drawn by the lake's scenic setting in the highlands and its still, mirror-like surface during the dry season, from which the lake's Birú name (meaning "clear reflections") is derived. Tourism infrastructure remains limited, consisting of a small number of guesthouses and informal catering establishments. The [[Gran Presa del Nord]] and the lake are the principal attractions, though the area's remoteness and limited transport infrastructure constrain visitor numbers.

The village's role as comarca capital provides a modest number of administrative and public sector employment positions. The municipal council maintains basic services, and the village has a health clinic and primary school serving the surrounding communities.

== History ==

The area around Vila de Llevant has been inhabited by the [[Birú people|indigenous Birú]] for centuries. The Birú maintained communities along the shores of Lake Iwarakí and in the surrounding highland valleys, relying on the lake for fishing and the valley floors for agriculture.

The village was established as the comarca capital during the colonial period, serving as an administrative node for the sparse population of the eastern highlands. Its location on the lake shore made it a natural focal point for the surrounding communities. The settlement remained small throughout the colonial and republican periods, with limited growth and minimal integration into the broader colonial economy.

The construction of the [[Gran Presa del Nord]] in the 1970s brought the lake under regulation as a reservoir, raising the water level and altering the shoreline. The project had limited direct economic impact on Vila de Llevant compared to [[Sant Jorge, Tramuntana|Sant Jorge]], which became the primary service centre for the CHB workforce, though the dam and reservoir did contribute to the village's minor tourism sector.

